+The Footvector Representation is a geometric generalization of Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) for curves and surfaces, defined by the vector pointing from a query point to its closest point (footpoint) on the shape [G]. Unlike standard SDFs which only provide scalar distance, this representation retains directional information, enabling exact offset surface computation for Constructive Solid Geometry (CSG) operations via fast iterative methods [S1, abstract only, unofficial]. It builds on the [Bézier Curve Signed Distance Function](5929-b-zier-curve-signed-distance-function.md) and differs from the [Spline Tube to Cylinder Mapping](10061-spline-tube-to-cylinder-mapping.md) by explicitly tracking the footpoint vector rather than mapping to a cylinder tube [S1, abstract only, unofficial]. It is chosen when precise geometric invariants and exact offsetting are required, though it poses implementation challenges for GPU shaders due to higher-order function mappings [S1, abstract only, unofficial].
 
